diagnosis_text:
A complete Phase 1 reachability baseline, Phase A infrastructure sweep (infra_sweep + l2_snapshot), Phase B OSPF and routing-table audit (ospf_snapshot plus direct FRR table inspection on router7, router16, router17, sshswitch, and mswitch0), targeted inter-VLAN ping probes across all major subnet pairs, and Phase D pressure sweep were all performed. No anomaly was found at any layer.

Specifically: all 15 end-hosts hold valid IPv4 addresses and default routes with zero gateway divergence. All nine OSPF routers are fully converged with 17 routes apiece, matching exactly the 17 distinct subnets declared across the topology (VLAN5, VLAN6, VLAN10, VLAN20, VLAN30, interconnect links, and two unoccupied VLANs). Every FRR routing table inspected shows correct OSPF-learned next-hops for every remote subnet and no static or null routes that could create a blackhole. Direct ping tests across the pairs not sampled by get_reachability — VLAN5↔VLAN10, VLAN5↔VLAN30, VLAN6↔VLAN10, VLAN10↔VLAN30 — all returned 0% packet loss. No ACL rules, no traffic-control shaping, no resource pressure, and no MAC-address conflicts were detected on any device.

The helpdesk complaint of "one whole group of machines erased from the network" is not corroborated by any observable evidence. The network is operating normally. The reported symptom may have been transient and self-corrected prior to this investigation, or may reflect a misdiagnosis (e.g., a host-side firewall or application-level issue not present in this topology).

Root cause: No fault present; network is fully operational at all inspected layers.
Fix: No corrective action required.
=== END ===